  "kind": "process",
  "title": "The global silver trade",
  "summary": "The silver mines of Potosí were discovered in 1545, and those of Zacatecas began operating under Spanish control in 1547–48, with mining expanded through forced labour. By 1600, 25,000 tons of silver had reached Spain, and commodity prices rose 400% over the 16th century. Across the Pacific, Manila galleons sailed from 1565 to 1815, carrying American silver toward Asia and bringing silk, spices and porcelain back. China needed silver after ending its ban on private maritime trade in 1567 and basing its currency on it.",
